## Owned producer draft

The private `_continue_owned_development` route constructs a fresh
`h1_owned_bootstrap_controller_development_v1.OwnedController`; it is not the
default continuation. Its six runtime leases remain live from before ready
publication through local/global release registration and N0 processing.
No controller handle is stored on BootstrapOwner or returned to the caller.
`live_controller_retained=false` describes that absence of a retained capability;
the private route has a local live instance during its operation. It cannot
rehydrate execution from closed genesis metadata.

The inherited base lease is permanently None after construction and inherited
`_check` always rejects. Independent named owned methods preserve the existing
parent, obligation, clock and Job transactions. All four nonblocking guards are
held in their original order across ready/release callbacks and execution.
The same PID/thread, owner operation/nonce, six original leases and lock bytes,
configuration and release heads are rechecked at effect boundaries. Lock reads
use each owned handle and require one regular single-link byte containing `0`.
Each lease's original handle, root and file identities are fixed separately.
The owner network object/identity, fixed clock/obligation roots and binding pins,
192-hour declaration and N0 cursors are also bound. Expected cursor changes are
made only by this transaction after its verified terminal transitions. Clock
start and intent pins are fixed at their corresponding owned publication steps.
All post-super constructor lookups are covered by cleanup, with leases captured
incrementally. The cleanup tuple is checked against the six fixed lease objects;
close identity-deduplicates independent original/runtime/root captures so a
detected omission from either cleanup tuple still releases all original leases.
N0 indices, horizon and clock scalars require exact integer types, including
rejection of Python bool/numerically equal float substitutions before effects.
Constructor-tail failure closes every captured original lease. An active Job
request must match the exact path and digest reconstructed from its ticket,
including at gates that precede request-file creation.

The single-use N0 scope reads retained global release, observes headroom, then
internally loads current source and derives its identity and predecessor.
The parent independently reloads that identity. Job creation, suspended-child
creation and child release each require the exact owned request context.
Whole-Job exit, source/snapshot rereads, numerical replay, parent outcome and
clock/obligation records retain their existing acceptance rules.
Before Job-directory creation the derived scratch cwd must fit Windows
CreateProcessW's MAX_PATH limit measured in UTF-16 code units. This explicit rejection preserves an
indeterminate attempt; it does not shorten or relocate a declared episode.

The owned-only route returns `pending_N_terminal_registration`; its four-record
prefix remains execution_indeterminate and disallows retry. The separate
registered route below adds local/global N0 metadata after controller closure.
Paired handoff remains absent. The owned route
is new R3 draft code requiring numerical synthetic checks and independent
pre-seal review. Its existence grants no native authority or resource admission.


## N0 terminal evidence preparation draft

The private `_prepare_N0_terminal` helper accepts no caller envelope, source key,
terminal pin or body. Under the original active BootstrapOwner operation it
rebuilds the retained four-record release prefix, derives the obligation binding
from controller genesis and the N0 terminal pin from its fixed file, then invokes
the existing historical `prepare(completed_hours=1)` numerical replay. The
returned object type alone is not a credential: the helper invokes the reader
internally and checks its declaration, pins, implementation, decision and audit.

The historical reader deliberately allows later recorded suffixes. Bootstrap
therefore adds an exact N0 protocol topology check around that preparation:
controller Job 000; clock 000; obligation 000 binding/job/terminal; source-parent
hour 000; parent events 001/002; anchors 000/001/002. Existing Job/worker views
bind the deeper prefix. The same observed file/directory identities and bytes,
source support identities, release heads and operation nonce must survive the
preparation. The candidate body retains a topology digest separately from the
scientific decision identity. Historical suffix flags are not relabelled.

Scratch is an opaque plain directory node with stable identity. Its contents
remain unaudited and outside scientific evidence; an 8 MiB headroom demand is
not a proved allocation bound. Normal temporary files need not be removed or
interpreted to prepare a numerical candidate.

This helper does not write local/global terminals and is not connected to the
default or owned continuation. Returned evidence is fresh at return only.
The internal evidence builder is also used by the registered route and registry
replay below. All formal/resource/native authority flags remain false.


## N0 local/global terminal registration draft

The private `_continue_registered_development` route runs the owned N0 route,
expires its scope and closes its leases, then retains local seq5 and the global
N0 terminal under the same original BootstrapOwner operation nonce. Default
bootstrap remains metadata-only. No caller supplies a terminal body or envelope.

Before the first attempt, the exact local terminal schema and a maximal global
sequence number determine record byte bounds. Every dynamic body field except
the fixed derived controller root is an exact 64-character SHA256; the same
body constructor is used for the bound and real records. Logical global debts
are two records for a claim, one for a release and zero after its N0 terminal;
a new claim still reserves three. These are logical limits, not physical resource
admission. Unresolved N execution remains indeterminate rather than being
rewritten as an accepted or infeasible terminal.

The writer holds the expected local/global row sequences, original nonce and
N0 observations across both retention callbacks. After local retention it
rebuilds the numerical audit and compares the full body before global writing.
After global retention it cross-replays the registry and rechecks observations
and operation bindings. Global metadata binds the claim, release, exact local
terminal head/body digest and evidence identity. A terminal never grants native
or paired-consumption authority.

Registry replay validates local seq5's canonical chain and exact numerical
body even for foreign episodes, retains its observations, and rechecks local
journal/genesis views and global rows after final numerical/source observations.
Unknown seq6+ and unknown global transitions are rejected. A foreign local seq5
without a global terminal gives no completion or reuse credit; reopening its own
BootstrapOwner still requires that local head to have been externally retained.

An externally retained local terminal without global commit is
`local_N_terminal_uncommitted`, recovery_required=true, retry_authorized=false.
Explicit `reconcile_N0_terminal` on a freshly reopened owner replays the same
local terminal and appends only the missing global record. It never runs N,
changes the local terminal or adopts a suffix beyond supplied external heads.
A committed N0 pair of journal records is `pending_post_N`; this is pending
business/Rref/Actual paired execution, not a paired research result.
`N0_numerical_evidence_audited` identifies the narrow numerical scope; the broad
`execution_artifacts_audited` remains false because scratch is opaque.

Tests separate zero-Job protocol fault injection with explicitly fake numerical
evidence from the full synthetic Windows Job/numerical registration and foreign
replay case. These draft checks do not prove real native authentication,
complete192/all-family support, resource admission or formal-run authority.
# Registry declaration binding (development addition)

An optional typed `RegistryBinding(path, sha256, split)` is included in the immutable
BootstrapDeclaration and study binding, hence in local and global journal
identities and foreign-episode replay. Constructor/reopen and the first attempt
freshly inspect the declaration against the obligation's exact split.
The study binding also retains the resolved global registry identity and split.
Full inspection repeats after the retained global claim, before continuation.
Owner checks compare the stable declaration bytes and file identity, including
after retention callbacks. A changed declaration cannot be adopted on reopen.

Absent binding retains the existing N-only development path. A present binding
is only pre-execution metadata: `registry_declaration_bound` does not imply
paired source correspondence, a validated current outage window or run authority.
Future paired entry must freshly call the registry and phase source validators;
no cached declaration view substitutes for those checks. No new native authority
is introduced here.



