## Phase manifest candidate binding (2026-10-11 open development addition)

An optional exact `phase_archive` requires the existing selector declaration.
The study binds only this common archive declaration. Each episode header stores
the full internally prepared canonical phase audit plus SHA; its proposal stores
only that SHA and the header genesis. Foreign replay prepares the exact foreign
ordinal and compares the selected sibling's complete obligation node with the
symbolic manifest. No caller-supplied candidate or SHA is a preparation credential.

The private per-handle cache keys family, ordinal, selector binding bytes and
archive bytes, and stores only canonical audit bytes plus digest. Cache mapping,
keys and entries are checked against the internally retained snapshot. Every
owner check recomputes both closed implementation fingerprints from fresh stable
dependency bytes. Cache misses bookend preparation and match those fingerprints
against the prepared audit. The cache saves projection work, never source checks.
New handles independently prepare their candidates; same-group arm ordinals are
not merged and receive no reuse or resource credit.

Before directory/header creation, the study header, full episode header, attempt
and claim are encoded and checked against the bootstrap journal cap. This is
separate from the phase archive event cap and proves no full runtime/storage bound.
Callbacks and later replay retain the existing fail-closed, no-automatic-retry
behavior. All failed bytes and uncommitted suffixes remain available for diagnosis.

This addition does not attach a phase owner, holder, selector producer or seq6.
The manifest may describe three lanes while the claim remains one arm-specific
symbolic N obligation. `phase_manifest_candidate_bound` reports declaration
binding only. Owner attachment, authenticated group execution roster, all-sibling
execution and normal reuse flags remain false. Integrated bootstrap development
and independent review remain open until the new tests and findings close.
